  "slug": "dynamics-eeg-d335",
  "summary": "Physics-structured digital twins are now fitted to EEG and multi-omics data, exposing where structural priors help and where they fall short.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of physics-structured dynamical models as surrogates for biological time series. Two preprints apply port-Hamiltonian formulations: a metriplectic port-Hamiltonian GNN fitted to PhysioNet motor imagery EEG reproduces avalanche criticality, and a multi-clock port-Hamiltonian GNN surrogate of mouse liver circadian multi-omics is stable and forecasts well. For the circadian model, network topology recovery is at chance, so trajectory fidelity does not imply mechanistic recovery. The RG-Flow transformer, which encodes scale-free dynamics, matches a vanilla transformer on EEG sleep staging (77.3% vs 77.0%) while recovering spectral exponent β out-of-sample. A separate information-theoretic preprint bounds perturbation prediction by the variance captured by the projection basis, with graph wavelets outperforming PCA. The common contribution is testing whether structural or scale-aware priors recover mechanism, not only predict trajectories.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-15"
}
